"type":"text","value":"We start by checking the current index. We can do this by using the ","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1,"offset":1027},"end":{"line":20,"column":69,"offset":1095}}},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"load_current_index_checked","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":69,"offset":1095},"end":{"line":20,"column":97,"offset":1123}}},{"type":"text","value":" function, like so:","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":97,"offset":1123},"end":{"line":20,"column":116,"offset":1142}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1,"offset":1027},"end":{"line":20,"column":116,"offset":1142}}},{"type":"code","lang":"rust","meta":null,"value":"let instruction_sysvar = unsafe { Instructions::new_unchecked(&self.accounts.sysvar_instructions.try_borrow_data()?) };\r\nlet index = instruction_sysvar.load_current_index();","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1,"offset":1146},"end":{"line":25,"column":4,"offset":1333}},"data":{"highlightId":"code-1"}},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"After this, we can check an instruction at a relative index using ","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1,"offset":1337},"end":{"line":27,"column":67,"offset":1403}}},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"load_instruction_at_checked","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":67,"offset":1403},"end":{"line":27,"column":96,"offset":1432}}},{"type":"text","value":". This time, we'll check the instruction immediately following the one performing the introspection, like so:","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":96,"offset":1432},"end":{"line":27,"column":205,"offset":1541}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1,"offset":1337},"end":{"line":27,"column":205,"offset":1541}}},{"type":"code","lang":"rust","meta":null,"value":"// We load the next instruction, which is the one we want to check for the correct input.\r\nlet instruction = instruction_sysvar.load_instruction_at(index as usize + 1)?;","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1,"offset":1545},"end":{"line":32,"column":4,"offset":1728}},"data":{"highlightId":"code-2"}},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Before going to the next step, we should ask ourselves what information is essential to prevent a malicious attack.","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1,"offset":1732},"end":{"line":34,"column":116,"offset":1847}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1,"offset":1732},"end":{"line":34,"column":116,"offset":1847}}},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"We usually start by checking if the program being used is the one we expect. In this example, we're introspecting another instruction from the system program, so we can do it like so:","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1,"offset":1851},"end":{"line":36,"column":184,"offset":2034}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1,"offset":1851},"end":{"line":36,"column":184,"offset":2034}}},{"type":"code","lang":"rust","meta":null,"value":"if instruction.get_program_id() != &pinocchio_system::ID {\r\n    return Err(ProgramError::InvalidInstructionData);\r\n}","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1,"offset":2038},"end":{"line":42,"column":4,"offset":2168}},"data":{"highlightId":"code-3"}},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Next, we check if the instruction is the one we expect. To do so, we compare the discriminator and the instruction data with the expected values. We start by creating an ","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1,"offset":2172},"end":{"line":44,"column":171,"offset":2342}}},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"instruction_data","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":171,"offset":2342},"end":{"line":44,"column":189,"offset":2360}}},{"type":"text","value":" variable and checking against it, like so:","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":189,"offset":2360},"end":{"line":44,"column":232,"offset":2403}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1,"offset":2172},"end":{"line":44,"column":232,"offset":2403}}},{"type":"code","lang":"rust","meta":null,"value":"let mut instruction_data = [0u8; 12];\r\ninstruction_data[0..4].copy_from_slice(&2u32.to_le_bytes());\r\ninstruction_data[4..12].copy_from_slice(&100_000_000u64.to_le_bytes());\r\n\r\nif instruction.get_instruction_data() != instruction_data {\r\n    return Err(ProgramError::InvalidInstructionData);\r\n}","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1,"offset":2407},"end":{"line":54,"column":4,"offset":2714}},"data":{"highlightId":"code-4"}},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"We can then perform more program-specific checks based on the logic of the instruction we are checking.","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1,"offset":2718},"end":{"line":56,"column":104,"offset":2821}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1,"offset":2718},"end":{"line":56,"column":104,"offset":2821}}},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"We can also check the accounts present in the introspected instruction. This step requires us to know the exact structure of the account struct, since we'll be requesting the data or pubkey of an account at a specific index, like this:","position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1,"offset":2825},"end":{"line":58,"column":236,"offset":3060}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1,"offset":2825},"end":{"line":58,"column":236,"offset":3060}}},{"type":"code","lang":"rust","meta":null,"value":"if !pubkey_eq(instruction.get_account_meta_at(0)?.key(), self.accounts.from.key()) {\r\n    return Err(ProgramError::InvalidAccountData);\r\n}","position":{"start":{"line":60,"column":1,"offset":3064},"end":{"line":64,"column":4,"offset":3216}},"data":{"highlightId":"code-5"}}],"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1,"offset":0},"end":{"line":65,"column":1,"offset":3218}}},"raw":"import { ArticleSection } from \"../../../../components/ArticleSection/ArticleSection\";\r\n\r\n# Instruction Introspection with Pinocchio\r\n\r\nPinocchio uses different types than the `solana_program` crate. This means we had to create a new SDK optimized for Pinocchio.\r\n\r\nWith the help of [Orion](https://x.com/BasedOrion_), we created a set of helpers that respected the \"original\"\r\nimplementation but made the `Instruction` sysvar — one of the most expensive sysvars to use — one of the most efficient.\r\n\r\nFor this reason, we can use *Instruction Introspection* in Pinocchio without installing any external crates and can access all the information we need by importing these functions:\r\n\r\n```rust\r\nuse pinocchio::sysvars::instructions::Instructions;\r\n```\r\n\r\n<ArticleSection name=\"How to use Introspection\" id=\"how-to-use-introspection\" level=\"h2\" />\r\n\r\nAs mentioned previously, Instruction Introspection simply deserializes the data from the `Instruction` sysvar account to provide all the data we need about our instructions.\r\n\r\nWe start by checking the current index. We can do this by using the `load_current_index_checked` function, like so:\r\n\r\n```rust\r\nlet instruction_sysvar = unsafe { Instructions::new_unchecked(&self.accounts.sysvar_instructions.try_borrow_data()?) };\r\nlet index = instruction_sysvar.load_current_index();\r\n```\r\n\r\nAfter this, we can check an instruction at a relative index using `load_instruction_at_checked`. This time, we'll check the instruction immediately following the one performing the introspection, like so:\r\n\r\n```rust\r\n// We load the next instruction, which is the one we want to check for the correct input.\r\nlet instruction = instruction_sysvar.load_instruction_at(index as usize + 1)?;\r\n```\r\n\r\nBefore going to the next step, we should ask ourselves what information is essential to prevent a malicious attack.\r\n\r\nWe usually start by checking if the program being used is the one we expect. In this example, we're introspecting another instruction from the system program, so we can do it like so:\r\n\r\n```rust\r\nif instruction.get_program_id() != &pinocchio_system::ID {\r\n    return Err(ProgramError::InvalidInstructionData);\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nNext, we check if the instruction is the one we expect. To do so, we compare the discriminator and the instruction data with the expected values. We start by creating an `instruction_data` variable and checking against it, like so:\r\n\r\n```rust\r\nlet mut instruction_data = [0u8; 12];\r\ninstruction_data[0..4].copy_from_slice(&2u32.to_le_bytes());\r\ninstruction_data[4..12].copy_from_slice(&100_000_000u64.to_le_bytes());\r\n\r\nif instruction.get_instruction_data() != instruction_data {\r\n    return Err(ProgramError::InvalidInstructionData);\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nWe can then perform more program-specific checks based on the logic of the instruction we are checking.\r\n\r\nWe can also check the accounts present in the introspected instruction. This step requires us to know the exact structure of the account struct, since we'll be requesting the data or pubkey of an account at a specific index, like this:\r\n\r\n```rust\r\nif !pubkey_eq(instruction.get_account_meta_at(0)?.key(), self.accounts.from.key()) {\r\n    return Err(ProgramError::InvalidAccountData);\r\n}\r\n```\r\n","highlightedCode":{"code-0":{"lang":"rust","hast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"pre","properties":{"class":"shiki Kiloshift","style":"background-color:#11141a;color:#f6f6f4","tabindex":"0"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"code","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"span","properties":{"class":"line"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"span","properties":{"style":"color:#F286C4"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"use"}]},{"type":"element","tagName":"span","properties":{"style":"color:#F6F6F4"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":" pinocchio"}]},{"type":"element","tagName":"span","properties":{"style":"color:#F286C4"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"::"}]},{"type":"element","tagName":"span","properties":{"style":"color:#F6F6F4"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"sysvars"}]},{"type":"element","tagNam